        // has occurred yet. In the mutant, pot can be >10 after a win.
        // Simple invariant: pot <= 10 for any sequence in reference? No, pot can be 20 if no win.
        // Actually, pot accumulates 10 each play without a win. So pot can be large. The only
        // difference is that after a win, reference pot=0, mutant pot>0. So we can assert that
        // pot is never >= 10 after a win. We can't detect "after a win" easily without external
        // event. Instead, we can check that pot is always a multiple of 10 at the start of each
        // play? In reference, pot%10==0 always? Yes, because wins reset to 0, and losses add 10.
        // In mutant, after win, pot can be 9 (pot - fee) because it transfers pot-FEE_AMOUNT and
        // doesn't reset. Pot was 10, minus 1 fee, so pot becomes 9. Then next play adds 10, pot=19.
        // So pot%10 can be 9. So we assert: pot % 10 == 0. In reference, always true. In mutant,
