        // So the rule: after one play, it is NOT the case that pot > 0 always? That's not an assertion.
        // Let's invert: a rule that reference always satisfies: if pot > 0 after a play, then the play was not a win.
        // But that's not directly testable. We need a boolean property.
        // Rethink: reference after a win: pot == 0. After a loss: pot == 10. Mutant after a win: pot == 10 (since pot was 0 + 10, then no reset). After a loss: pot == 10 also. So after one play, reference pot is either 0 or 10, mutant pot is always 10.
        // So reference satisfies: pot is either 0 or 10. Mutant violates: pot can be 10, but not 0. Wait, mutant pot is always 10 after first play? If pot starts 0, play adds 10 -> pot=10. Win does not reset, so pot remains 10. So mutant pot is always 10 after a single play. So reference pot is 0 or 10, mutant pot is 10 only. Thus reference satisfies "pot <= 10" but that holds for mutant too. The difference: reference can have pot == 0, mutant never has pot == 0. So the rule "pot == 0 OR pot == 10" is true for reference, but also true for mutant (since 10 is in the set). Not a violation.
        // We need the mutant to violate something. Since mutant never resets, after a win, pot is 10 instead of 0. So reference satisfies "pot != 10 after a win", but we don't know if a win occurred.
